 2-year median of 26%, and skew is flat. They are fairly priced, not cheap, so price gives no reason to add. I couldn't check actual quotes, strikes or realized volatility.\n- **Headline demand is up, but the real picture is weaker.**\n  - Pellham Power plans 2027 capex up 18% and Norvane up 12%, mostly substations and transformers.\n  - Data-centre grid connection requests should grow 9% in 2027. That is slower than in 2026.\n- **Financing is the main risk, and it falls on your biggest customer.**\n  - Debt markets are open but pricier: utility bond spreads widened 30bp in Q3 to 165bp. Norvane priced a C$1.1B bond comfortably.\n  - Equity markets are weak. Pellham withdrew its C$2.4B equity offering after demand fell short. It is cutting 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and deferring two substation programmes \"until financing improves.\"\n  - Pellham is 35% of VLTX's utility orders, and utilities are 62% of its orders overall. The +18% capex headline therefore overstates what VLTX will actually sell.\n\n**What would make me add:**\n- A successful Pellham equity re-launch, or the deferred programmes restarting.\n- Spreads staying near or below 165bp.\n- Option quotes showing LEAPS implied volatility at or below the median.\n\nPaying 27% vol for LEAPS means betting on a demand thesis whose largest buyer is cutting orders.\n\nSources: n2.r1 (demand), n3.r1 (financing), n4.r1 (pricing).","ttft_ms":2431,"type":"result","duration_ms":41013,"uuid":"53dddf03-4908-43b7-b100-51cd9fc5be55","ttft_stream_ms":1465,"time_to_request_ms":352,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":27,"system_prompt":29,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":8,"input_attachments":71,"input_hooks":13,"conversation_append":15,"transcript_persist":21,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":39,"init_frame_send":6,"engine_pickup":2,"loop_entry":14,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":42,"query_setup":1,"model_gates":0,"tool_schema_build":13,"message_normalization":